You will compare one known error in a data agent's work against a ranked list of items produced by a reviewer. Your job is to decide whether any item in the list identifies this known error, and if so, which one.

An item identifies the known error only if both of these hold:
- It is about the same thing: the same table, and the same column, computation, filter, or join.
- It points to the same problem: what the item says is wrong, assumed, or missing is the same as what went wrong in the known error. It does not matter whether the item is phrased as an assumption the agent made ("the agent assumes ...") or as a problem in the work ("this does not match ..."); judge by meaning.

An item does not count if it only says that something may be wrong without saying what, such as "this column may be computed incorrectly". An item about the same column but a different problem does not count.

If more than one item identifies the error, report the highest-ranked one. If you are unsure, say so rather than guessing.

Return a JSON object with:
- "verdict": "match", "no_match", or "unsure".
- "rank": the position of the matching item (1 is the top), or null.
- "quote": the exact words from the matching item that show it identifies the error, or null.
- "reason": one short sentence.

=== Known error ===
Table: jira__team_performance_dashboard
What the agent's work assumed: avg_weekly_activity_score 的平均值为空时补 0 就行
What was actually required or true: 不补 0，保留空值

=== Reviewer's ranked list for this table ===
[
  {
    "rank": 1,
    "item": {
      "problem": "The dashboard starts from all users rather than an inner match between user metrics and users.",
      "about": "requirement",
      "evidence_in_work": "from staging.stg_jira__user u left join intermediate.int_jira__user_metrics m",
      "source": "Join user metrics to users on user_id",
      "reason": "The output has 6,000 users while user metrics has 4,374, potentially expanding the required scope."
    }
  },
  {
    "rank": 2,
    "item": {
      "problem": "Overall performance scores are fixed at zero instead of applying the component scoring rules.",
      "about": "requirement",
      "evidence_in_work": "0::integer overall_performance_score",
      "source": "Quality (20%): ... 5 points ... else",
      "reason": "Even unavailable quality data should enter the specified fallback scoring rather than force a zero total."
    }
  },
  {
    "rank": 3,
    "item": {
      "problem": "User growth analysis is omitted and real project, issue-type, and reported-issue counts are replaced with zero.",
      "about": "requirement",
      "evidence_in_work": "0::bigint projects_contributed_to,0::bigint issue_types_worked_on ... 0::bigint leadership_indicator",
      "source": "Consider issues where the user is assignee, reporter, or creator",
      "reason": "These fields should contain meaningful activity history even when all_projects joins miss collaboration data."
    }
  },
  {
    "rank": 4,
    "item": {
      "problem": "days_since_first_issue is always NULL rather than derived from the earliest related issue.",
      "about": "requirement",
      "evidence_in_work": "null::double days_since_first_issue",
      "source": "days_since_first_issue ... days ... first_issue_date",
      "reason": "User tenure information is entirely absent."
    }
  },
  {
    "rank": 5,
    "item": {
      "problem": "Collaboration metrics are never built from recent issues, links, and comments.",
      "about": "requirement",
      "evidence_in_work": "null::bigint total_assigned_issues ... null::bigint unique_collaborators_on_issues",
      "source": "Build team_collaboration_metrics from issues created in the last 90 days",
      "reason": "The required processing is absent, although ordinary project IDs may make the final all_projects joins NULL anyway."
    }
  },
  {
    "rank": 6,
    "item": {
      "problem": "Sprint history, sprint metadata, and active story-point fields are unused.",
      "about": "requirement",
      "evidence_in_work": "null::bigint sprint_count ... null::decimal(38,3) total_story_points_delivered",
      "source": "Build sprint_participation_analysis from recent sprints ... raw.issue_sprint_history",
      "reason": "Required sprint processing is omitted and would fail to populate any legitimate matching project group."
    }
  },
  {
    "rank": 7,
    "item": {
      "problem": "Missing average close-time metrics are converted to zero without a specified fallback.",
      "about": "requirement",
      "evidence_in_work": "coalesce(m.avg_close_time_days,0)::decimal(18,0)",
      "source": "Set avg_daily_activity_score and avg_weekly_activity_score from average close time days",
      "reason": "Missing duration information is presented as a genuine zero activity score."
    }
  },
  {
    "rank": 8,
    "item": {
      "problem": "performance_tier is hard-coded rather than classified from the computed score.",
      "about": "requirement",
      "evidence_in_work": "'Needs Improvement'::varchar performance_tier",
      "source": "Top Performer (score ≥85), Strong Performer (≥70), Average Performer (≥50), else Needs Improvement",
      "reason": "The implementation cannot reflect improved scores if data produces them."
    }
  },
  {
    "rank": 9,
    "item": {
      "problem": "poor_estimation is fixed false rather than evaluated from estimate accuracy.",
      "about": "requirement",
      "evidence_in_work": "false poor_estimation",
      "source": "poor_estimation is true when estimate accuracy ratio > 2",
      "reason": "Eligible poor-estimation signals can never be surfaced."
    }
  },
  {
    "rank": 10,
    "item": {
      "problem": "The required score, project-name, and user-name ordering is omitted.",
      "about": "requirement",
      "evidence_in_work": "left join intermediate.int_jira__user_metrics m using(user_id)",
      "source": "ordered by overall_performance_score descending, then project_name, then user_display_name",
      "reason": "Direct SQL presentation differs from the dashboard contract."
    }
  }
]
